smolvm: keep time across a restore on a host with another counter rate

A VM checkpointed on one host can be restored on another whose system
counter runs at a different rate: 24 MHz on Apple silicon, 1 GHz on
Armv8.6+ servers. The kernel fixes the counter rate at boot, so after such
a move every clock would run fast or slow by the ratio of the two rates.

Re-read CNTFRQ_EL0 in the virtual timer interrupt. When it no longer
matches, keep time in boot-rate ticks by reading the virtual counter
through a mapping, ticks = base + (hw - hw_base) * boot_rate / rate, that
continues exactly from the previous reading. The switch happens with the
timekeeper locked so every clock stays continuous and monotonic. Event
deadlines and the delay loop convert boot-rate ticks back to hardware
ticks, and each CPU recomputes its event stream divider. The vDSO cannot
apply the mapping, so user space falls back to the clock_gettime()
syscall after the first rate change.

The mapping is the identity until the rate first changes, so a VM that
never moves behaves exactly as before.
---
